**Status:** D5 is done for the engine, the FFI fence and the three semantic/stack/register kernels. The AST walker cannot be gated yet, and that needs a ruling before D6.

## Blocker: the AST walker drops `:yin.k/gate`

- `ast_walker.cljc` `cesk-return` rebuilds `ASTWalkerVM` through the positional constructor. That drops every key that is not a record field, so `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/closes` and `:yin.k/issued` vanish at the first step.
- Gated cursor, close and FFI rows on the walker therefore ran ungated; I confirmed this by running them red.
- Fixing it means adding record fields in `ast_walker.cljc`, outside my diff boundary, so I stopped.
- My walker `put-request` call site is correct. It reads the gate off `state`, and a unit test covers that function directly.
- The gated rows run on the other three kernels only (`gated-kernels` in the test file). Ungated rows run on all four.
- This affects D6's claim that the gate is complete on all four kernels, and needs a ruling (a record-field edit, or carrying these keys some other way).

## What changed

**`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`**
- `handle-effect :stream/cursor`: under any gate mode it calls the new `handle-cursor-unminted`, which makes no handle call and is not blocked. It installs `{:stream-id id :yin.k/unminted {:origin :dao.stream/oldest :seq n}}`, with `n` taken from the id counter. Ungated is unchanged.
- New `apply-mint [state cell-id position]`: replaces the cell with `(vm/cursor-entry stream-id position)`. It refuses a cell that is not unminted and refuses in `:exporting` and `:ended`.
- Close queue: under `:running`, `handle-close` appends `{:stream-id id :yin.k/issue n}` to `:yin.k/closes` and returns nil, with no handle call. Ungated is unchanged.
- New `apply-close [state stream-id issue]`: removes that one record and refuses in `:exporting` and `:ended`.
- Issue counter: `:yin.k/issued` is machine-only, and `:yin.k/issue n` stamps every gated parked `:put` entry and every close record.
- `check-wait-set`: under `:running` the ordinary sweep no longer polls `:put` or `:next` entries; they are kept in place, so the sweep makes zero handle calls. Ungated sweeps are unchanged.
- The poll-branch line is reflowed to 80 columns or fewer. Line 361 is still over 80, but it predates this work.

**`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc`**
- New `put-request [state call-in request]`. With no gate it calls `apply2/put-request!`. With any gate it answers `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/full}` and makes no handle call.

**Four kernels** (`semantic.cljc`, `debruijn/stack.cljc`, `debruijn/register.cljc`, `ast_walker.cljc`)
- The one `apply2/put-request!` at each `:ffi-call` site is now `ffi/put-request`. Nothing else at the site changed.

**`test/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc`**
- Added D5 tests covering:
  - sweep rows (gated zero calls and entries kept; ungated retry as before);
  - cursor rows 1–9, per kernel where the ruling says so;
  - close rows, including issue order, `apply-close`, the forged reference and ungated unchanged;
  - FFI rows on semantic, stack and register: zero appends, and a wait-set identical to the ungated `full` branch with no issue stamp, plus the sweep leaving it alone;
  - a unit test of `put-request` in every gate mode.

